## 1. Introduction
Frogs, particularly their legs, are consumed as food by humans in many parts of the world. Originally, they were supplied from local wild populations, but overexploitation led to a diminution in the supply. As a result, frog farming and a global trade in frogs have been developed [1,2]. The main importing countries are France, Belgium, Luxembourg and the United States, and the chief exporters are Indonesia and China [1]. The annual global trade of the American bullfrog Rana catesbeiana,  which is mostly farmed in China, varies between 1200 and 2400 t [2].
Many researchers have extracted and characterised collagens from marine and freshwater fish [3–6]. Environmental temperature affects collagen stability. Namely, Collagen molecules in solution denature close to the upper limit of the physiological temperature or the maximum body temperature of the animal from which the collagen is extracted [7]. Hence, the thermostability of collagens from deep-sea fish is usually lower than that of collagens from freshwater fish. Collagens from mammals, such as calf and porcine, exhibit higher denaturation temperature $\mathrm{(T_{d})}$ than those from fish. However, few reports were on the collagens from amphibians [8].
Sathya Sai Kumar et al. once utilised collagen from frog (Rana tigerina Daudin) skin as a novel substrate in cell culture [9].
Frog resembles freshwater fish in terms of conserving body water effectively. However, the excretory system of frog, similar to that of mammals, comprises two kidneys that remove nitrogenous products from the blood [10]. As the first vertebrates connecting water and land, frogs must have evolved certain specialisations. Accordingly, we intend to explore whether or not the thermostability of collagens from amphibians is between that of collagens from fish and mammals. Research on the properties of frog collagens is crucial to understand the nature of amphibian collagens and identify the influential factors of collagen thermostability. Therefore, this study aims to isolate and characterise acid-soluble collagen (ASC) and pepsin-solubilised collagen (PSC) from the skin of frog (Rana nigromaculata).
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Raw materials
Frogs were manually skinned in biological laboratory in Shangdong Institute of Freshwater Fisheries and then transported in ice to Lianyungang. The samples were washed with chilled tap water, placed in polyethylene bags and then stored at $-25^{\circ}C$ until use.
## 2.2. Chemical reagents
All reagents were of analytical grade. A high-molecular-weight marker kit was purchased from Sigma–Aldrich Chemical Company (St. Louis, Mo., USA).
## 2.3. Proximate analysis of frog skin
The moisture, protein, fat and ash contents of the samples were determined according to the method of AOAC [11]. The hydroxyproline content of the samples was determined using the colorimetric method recommended by the ISO after the material was hydrolysed in 6 M hydrochloric acid for $^{8\mathrm{h}}$ at $110^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ [12].
## 2.4. Preparation of acid-soluble collagen (ASC) and pepsin-solubilised collagen (PSC)
All preparation procedures for ASC and PSC were performed at $4^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ ASC was isolated and purified following the method described by Duan et al. [13].
After extracting collagen with $0.5\mathsf{M}$ acetic acid, the insoluble components were solubilised with 10 vols of $0.5\mathrm{M}$ acetic acid containing $0.1\%\ \mathrm{(w/v)}$ pepsin $(1/10000$ , Sigma–Aldrich) for $_{3\mathrm{~d~}}$ The solution was centrifuged at $20,000\times g$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ at $4^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . The supernatant was salted out by adding NaCl to obtain a final concentration of $2.5\mathsf{M}$ in the presence of 0.05 M Tris. The resulting precipitate was collected by centrifugation at $20,000\times g$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ and then dissolved in 5 vols of $0.5\mathsf{M}$ acetic acid. Salting-out and solubilisation were repeated twice. The resulting solution was dialysed against $0.1\ensuremath{\mathrm{M}}$ acetic acid and distilled water for $3\mathrm{~d~}$ . The dialysis solution was changed five times every day, and the sample was lyophilised (PSC from frog skin).
## 2.5. Sodium dodecyl sulphate–polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (SDS–PAGE)
Electrophoresis was conducted on $7.5\%$ gels by using the method described by Laemmli [14]. High-molecular-weight markers (Sigma–Aldrich) were used to estimate the molecular weights of the proteins.
## 2.6. Amino acid analysis and UV–vis spectra
Frog skin ASC was hydrolysed in 6 M HCl at $110^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ without oxygen [15]. The hydrolysates were investigated using a Hitachi 835-50 amino acid analyser. The UV–vis absorption spectra of the collagen were recorded by a Shimadzu spectrophotometer (Model UV-754). ASC samples at 1 mg  were dissolved in $2\mathrm{mL}$ of $0.5\mathrm{M}$ acetic acid, and the collagen solutions were centrifuged at $5000\times g$ for $10\mathrm{{min}}$ at $4^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . The absorbance of the clarified samples was determined at different wavelengths (from $190\mathrm{nm}$ to $450\mathrm{nm}$ ) to obtain the UV–vis spectra of each sample.
## 2.7. Determination of denaturation temperature
$\mathrm{T_{d}}$ was measured according to the method described by Duan et al. [13]. $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ was determined as the temperature at which the change in viscosity was half completed.
## 2.8. Peptide mapping
The peptide profiles of fish skin collagens were mapped according to the method of Laemmli [14]. High-molecular-weight markers (Sigma Chemical Co., St. Louis, Mo., USA) were used to estimate the molecular weight of the proteins.
## 2.9. Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy (FTIR)
The FTIR spectra of ASC and PSC from frog skin were recorded on a TENSOR-27 FTIR system (Bruker Optics, Inc., Massachusetts, USA).
## 2.10. Inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS)
Inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (Bruker ICP-MS M90) (Bruker Optics, Inc., Massachusetts, USA) was used to determine the content of Pb, Hg, and As. The collagens were dissolved in distilled water by heating at $35^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , followed by cooling at ambient temperature. The ICP-MS parameters were as follows: applied coolant gas flow rate of $18\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{min}$ ; auxiliary gas flow of $1.0\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{min}$ ; carrier gas flow rate of $0.26\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{min}$ ; sampling depth of $6.5\mathrm{{mm}}$ and power of $1.4\mathrm{kW}$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Proximate composition of frog skin
Table 1 shows the proximate analyses of frog skin. The moisture, protein, fat and ash contents of frog skin were $73.99\%,24.30\%,0.54\%$ and $1.81\%$ , respectively. The compositions of frog skin were similar to those of silver carp and cod skins [6].
Accordingly, the hydroxyproline-to-protein ratios of frog and calf skins were $8.73\%$ and $10.16\%$ , respectively. The hydroxyproline contents of carp and cod skins were $7.69\%$ and $6.77\%$ , respectively [6]. Hydroxyproline stabilises the triple-stranded collagen helix because of its hydrogen bonding ability through its OH group [16]. Hydroxyproline contents of the skins increases in the order of cod, silver carp and frog, which are deep-sea fish, freshwater fish and amphibian, respectively. This finding further proved that hydroxyproline content is dependent on species, living environment and body temperature [17].
## 3.2. Preparation of ASC and PSC
The yields of ASC and PSC from frog skin were $1.83\%$ and $19.59\%$ ， respectively, on a dry weight basis. The yield of PSC from frog skin was higher by more than 10 times than that of ASC. Fish collagens are usually extracted using acidic solutions without the aid of pepsin. The yields of ASC from Japanese sea bass, chub mackerel and bullhead shark were approximately $51.4\%$ , $49.8\%$ and $50.1\%$ , respectively (dry weight) [18]. However, the frog skin collagens exhibited a low solubility in $0.5\mathrm{M}$ acetic acid; thus, pepsin proteolysis was performed. It suggests that the telopeptide region of the collagen molecule is highly cross-linked [19]. The extractability of PSC significantly improved with the addition of pepsin. Pepsin can cleave the cross-linked regions at the telopeptide can be cleaved without damaging the integrity of the triple helix [20].
## 3.3. Denaturation temperature
The transition curves of the $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ of the frog skin collagens are shown in Fig. 1. The collagens were dissolved in $0.1\ensuremath{\mathrm{M}}$ acetic acid, and the $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ was determined by measuring viscosity. The $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ of the collagens was defined as the temperature at which the change in viscosity was half completed. The collagens were heated at different temperatures. The relative viscosities of ASC and PSC were almost stable from $5^{\circ}C$ to $15^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and then slightly decreased at $15^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ to $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . The viscosity rapidly decreased from $25^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ and the fractional changes of relative viscosity were almost zero at $36^{\circ}C$ for the two collagens. The transition curves of ASC and PSC were similar, and their $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ values were approximately $31.5^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ .
The $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ of collagens from cold water fish is generally lower than those of collagens from warm water fish. The $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ values of collagens from cod $(15.0^{\circ}\mathsf C)$ , brown backed toadfish $(28.0^{\circ}\mathsf C)$ , carp $(28.0^{\circ}\mathsf C)$ , grass carp $(28.4^{\circ}\mathsf C)$ were below $30^{\circ}\mathsf C$ [7,13,21–23]. The $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ values of ASC and PSC from frog skin were higher than those of collagens from fish. This result is consistent with the habitat of frog, i.e. partly in water and partly on land. Li et al.reported that the $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ of bullfrog skin collagen is $30.3^{\circ}\mathsf C$ [8]. Collagens from amphibians exhibit higher thermal stability than those from some marine and freshwater species. In addition, the $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ values of ASC and PSC from frog skin are lower than those of calf skin ASC $(40.8^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ [24]. The $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ values of collagens from frog skin are closer to those from freshwater fish collagen than to those from mammals.
## 3.4. Amino acid composition and UV–vis spectra
The amino acid compositions (expressed residues per 1000 total residues) of frog skin ASC, calf skin collagen and carp skin collagen are shown in Table 2.
Similar to other collagens, ASC from frog skin was rich in glycine (346 residues/1000 residues). The hydroxyproline and proline (imino acid) contents of frog skin ASC, calf skin collagen and carp skin collagen were 201 residues/1000 residues, 215 residues/1000 residues and 190 residues/1000 residues, respectively [13,25]. The imino acid content of frog skin collagen was higher than that of carp skin ASC but lower than that of calf skin collagen. High imino acid content correlates with high $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ and high collagen thermostability [26]. The $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ value of frog ASC $(31.5^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ was between those of fish skin collagen (cod skin collagen, $18^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ and carp skin collagen, $29^{\circ}\mathsf C$ 0 and calf skin collagen $(40.8^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}})$ . This result indicates that the thermostability of collagen gradually increases from fish, amphibians and mammals. The result of amino acid analysis was in accordance with that of $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ analysis.
The UV–vis spectra of ASC and PSC showed no absorption at $280\mathrm{nm}$ . This result is consistent with a previous report [8]. ASC and PSC showed positive peaks at 220 and $217\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively (figure not shown).
## 3.5. Electrophoresis
ASC and PSC from frog skin, along with collagen from carp skin, were analysed by polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis using $7.5\%$ gels.
As presented in Fig. 2, ASC and PSC consisted of $\upalpha$ chains and their dimer $\mathbf{\dot{\upbeta}}$ chains), as well as small amounts of $\upgamma$ components. The SDS-PAGE patterns of ASC and PSC were similar, showing one band between 205 and $116\mathrm{kDa}$ and another at approximately $116\mathrm{kDa}$ . The density of 1 was higher than that of $\upalpha2$ chain, which was similar to the density distribution of carp skin collagen and bullfrog skin PSC [8]. Hence, frog skin collagens may have the structure of $(\upalpha1)_{2}\upalpha2$ . Two bands of $\upbeta$ chains with different molecular masses were observed in the SDS-PAGE of frog skin collagens, which may indicated the existence of $\upbeta11$ and $\upbeta22$ . The $\upgamma$ chains of ASC indicated that the collagens exhibited many intramolecular and intermolecular cross-linkages.
## 3.6. Peptide mapping
Pepsin digestion was conducted following the method described by Zhang et al. [6]. Frog skin ASC digested by pepsin at $30^{\circ}\mathsf C$ was subjected to SDS-PAGE. As shown in Fig. 3, $\upbeta$ and $\propto2$ chain were degraded after $30\mathrm{min}$ of digestion. However, the sample was markedly degraded at $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . As enzyme treatment proceeded, $\upalpha$ and $\upbeta$ components of the heated samples were gradually degraded into short peptides. The SDS-PAGE pattern showed that the $\propto2$ chain of the heated samples almost disappeared within $2\mathrm{h}$ , whereas subunit $\propto1$ was resistant to digestion. When the digestion was conducted for $5\mathrm{h}$ , peptide fragments with MW values of 97, 66 and $36\mathrm{kDa}$ were the main products. The peptide profile of frog skin ASC differed from that of carp skin ASC digested by pepsin under the same condition [27]. This result can be attributed to the different primary structures of the two collagens.
## 3.7. FTIR
The FTIR spectra of frog skin ASC and PSC are shown in Fig. 4. Despite their different absorption intensities, ASC and PSC from frog skin showed similar spectral positions. The intensity of the infrared absorption was related to the transition probability and the size of the dipole moment change in the molecules. High absorption intensity meant high transition probability and dipole moment change. The electronic transitions of the $_\mathrm{X-H}$ groups $\mathrm{(X=C,N,}$ O) from frog skin ASC were simpler and possessed higher dipole moment changes than those of the same groups from frog skin PSC.
The amide A band positions of ASC and PSC were found at 3295 and $3292\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively. The amide A band was associated with $\mathsf{N{\mathrm{-}}H}$ stretching frequency. A free $\mathsf{N{\mathrm{-}}H}$ stretching vibration occurs at $3400\ c m^{-1}{-}3440\ c m^{-1}$ [28]. When the $\mathsf{N{\mathrm{-}}H}$ group of a peptide is involved in a hydrogen bond, the position is shifted to a low frequency, usually $3300\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ [8]. Thus, hydrogen bonds existed in frog skin ASC and PSC. The amide B band positions of ASC and PSC were observed at 2949 and $2950\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively, which can be related to the asymmetrical stretch of $\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ [29].
The amide I band positions of ASC and PSC were detected at approximately 1632 and $1630\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively, fitting well with the range of $1625\mathrm{cm}^{-1}-1690\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for general amide I band position. The amide II band positions of ASC and PSC were detected at 1548 and $1544\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively. Compared with the normal absorption range of the amide II band position $1550{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}-1600{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1},$ ), the position shifted to a low frequency, which also showed hydrogen bonds existed in frog skin ASC and PSC. The absorption between the 1236 and $1452\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ bands (amide III) demonstrates the presence of a helical structure and suggests the helical arrangement of frog skin collagens, with the ASC and PSC absorption ratios at 1425 and $1421\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively [30]. The FTIR indicated that the collagens retained their helical structures.
## 3.8. Inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS)
ICP-MS (Bruker Co.), which is faster and more sensitive than atomic absorption techniques, can detect metals and several nonmetals at concentrations as low as one part per trillion. The contents of Hg, Pb and As in collagens of frog skin were shown in Table 3, were significantly lower than the standard content in China. The results are indicated that heavy metal ions and other harmful elements did not accumulate in frog skin.
## 4. Conclusions
ASC and PSC were isolated from frog skin. The yield of PSC was higher than that of ASC. The electrophoresis results showed that both collagens were type I. The denaturation temperature $\left(T_{\mathrm{d}}\right)$ was approximately $31.5^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ ASC and PSC shared similar properties. The hydroxyproline content of frog skin collagen was between those of collagens from freshwater fish skin and calf skin. The results indicated that the properties of frog skin collagen were closer to those of collagen from freshwater fish than those of mammal collagen. Overall, the frog skin collagens can potentially be used as biomaterials such as collagen scoff.
